{define: DaddA base-fret 1 frets x 0 2 3 2 5} 
{define: Dsus4 base-fret 1 frets x x 0 2 3 3} 
{define: F#m base-fret 1 frets x x 4 2 2 2} 
{define: Gadd base-fret 1 frets 3 2 0 0 3 3} 
{t: DARCY FARROW – John Denver }
{st: (Gillete/Campbell) }
{artist: John Denver }
{composer: John Denver }


https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Itfg1DDAFmo&ab  (Capo 3)

TIP:  John fingerpicks the opening but it can be emulated with chords.  It looks like a mess but isn’t too hard as long as you can get the (DaddA) down.  It’s all a sequence consisting of the combination of a variation of (D) and (G).  So, start with (DaddA) by forming regular (D) and extending your pinky over to the 1st string of the 5th fret.  Then slide the pinky down to the 3rd fret to form (Dsus4) for one strum and then lift off to get back to (D).  (Gadd) is just (G) with your pinky added to the same position, 1st string/3rd fret.  Then remove it to get to (G) for one strum and then to (D).  Next comes another bar of (D) and then an (F#m) and then a quick movement from (DaddA) back to (Dsus4) and to (A).  Repeat the first part again and finish with alternating strums of (G), (A), and (D).  This sequence repeats in the instrumental bridge.

TIP:  If transposing, play the transposed (DaddA) and add the 5th note as needed.

TIP:  The entire sequence of chords above is played again in the verse.  I’ve mostly shown just the  majors but if you’re comfortable with the sequence you can play it that way.
 
TIP:  In the second line of each verse there’s that cumbersome move from (F#m) to (DaddA).  You can play regular (A) but the (DaddA) does sound better.
  
     
